  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on an online survey evaluating a single street redesign scenario rather than real-world post-construction perceptions.",
      "what": "A web-based survey of 417 adults evaluated participant perceptions of a proposed street redesign for cycling across three formats: a video of current conditions, a static 2D plan, and a dynamic interactive 3D model. Women and younger respondents reported greater difficulty interpreting the 2D plan. Participants rated the proposed redesign as safer and more appealing when presented as a 3D model compared to the 2D plan, a difference that was most pronounced among individuals who struggled to read the 2D drawing.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that standard 2D planning drawings systematically understate the perceived benefits of bicycle infrastructure projects for specific demographic groups who find technical plans harder to interpret."
    }
  },
